@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing
@using BlazorCompose.Site.Layout

@*
    NotFoundPage, not the <NotFound> fragment. Router.NotFound is [Obsolete] in .NET 10, and
    Router.RenderNotFound only falls back to it when NotFoundPage is null. NotFoundPage is rendered
    through the <Found> template below -- Router calls Found(new RouteData(NotFoundPage, ...)) -- so
    RouteView's DefaultLayout applies to it and no LayoutView or [Layout] attribute is needed.

    This makes a link click to an unknown path render the same component that a hard request for
    that path is served as 404.html, so the two cannot drift apart.

    Razor naming a hand-written C# component is fine; the reverse -- Component<T>() naming a
    Razor-generated type declared in this same project -- is reported as BC3012, because source
    generators cannot observe each other's output. A referenced project's .razor type is fine.
*@
<Router AppAssembly="@typeof(Program).Assembly" NotFoundPage="@typeof(Pages.NotFoundPage)">
    <Found Context="routeData">
        <RouteView RouteData="@routeData" DefaultLayout="@typeof(MainLayout)" />
    </Found>
</Router>
